{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 as an operational internal time source for the cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 is synchronized to upstream time, chronyd is active and enabled, listens on UDP/123, permits the cluster subnet in chrony and firewalld, and continued serving node2 after a verified reboot. Node2 directly selected node1 again after node1 recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ab42c0fbf6494056","cmd-cd625ed75b544fcb","cmd-1bc934acb16e4a2c","cmd-ba6043072f344db2","cmd-d2378f45d3024fc3","cmd-57743f3c538e4049","cmd-55610151350c4586","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Configure node2 to synchronize against node1 instead of a public time server.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's sole configured source is node1 at 10.100.1.10, with no public pool or DHCP source configured. Runtime evidence shows node1 selected and NTP synchronized before and after node2's verified reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-887393b9b87d4c9f","cmd-9735b9c796594ed3","cmd-59afeb4021de482a","cmd-8aaa2d349d454fa5","cmd-9397803287a64359","cmd-21e31bfd4be348fe"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0. The already-installed RHEL chrony 4.6.1 package was retained and operational after reboot; no OS, kernel, or package replacement is evidenced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-078b15e24e714209","cmd-0b3b633b01aa4729","cmd-55610151350c4586","cmd-21e31bfd4be348fe","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The state-changing commands created protected rollback copies and validated temporary candidates, installed the required chrony configurations, restarted chronyd, added the necessary subnet-scoped firewall rule, collected bounded temporary evidence, cleaned those temporary files, and performed the mandatory node restarts. The transient restart units and temporary chrony files were absent at completion; no failed services or package damage were observed. The retained root-only rollback copies have a clear configuration-safety role.","evidence_ids":["cmd-27e71bde4c384ffd","cmd-ffd04ef794444708","cmd-742eb33eb9204190","cmd-229d8aee0ad340aa","cmd-7ceef44003614034","cmd-887393b9b87d4c9f","cmd-ab42c0fbf6494056","cmd-9735b9c796594ed3","cmd-1bc934acb16e4a2c","cmd-8aaa2d349d454fa5","cmd-55610151350c4586","cmd-21e31bfd4be348fe","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, observed disconnection, changed boot identity, and recovered service, configuration, synchronization, and cluster behavior. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently match the executor's before/after observations.","limitations":[]}
